<!--
  BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.BlockAndInstance
-->
<block
  xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
  xsi:schemaLocation="http://blockml.org/bml https://blockml.org/schemas/bml.xsd
                      "
  xmlns:bml="org.blockml.bml.annotation.BmlAnnotation"
  xmlns:core="org.blockml.bml.core"
  xmlns:def="org.blockml.bml.definition"
  xmlns:doc="org.blockml.bml.documentation">

  <name>BlockAndInstance</name>
  <type>doc:BlockAndInstance</type>
  <baseType>core:Documentation</baseType>
  <is>A guide to Block vs instance</is>

  <documentation>
    The closest familiar model: a Block is a declarative semantic Class; a Block Instance is an
    Object. This is a semantic analogy — not a mandate for runtime OOP. The compiler may represent
    definitions and instances as plain data; the parser nonetheless distinguishes block documents
    (classes) from composition children (objects).

    Block (definition):
    • .bml file · BlockDefinition
    • FQN · members · composition schema
    • Describes shape and behaviour
    • No runtime · no address

    Block Instance (in composition):
    • Element such as ac:Wall with bml:id and member values
    • typeFqn + bml:id + member values
    • One concrete occurrence
    • May become a Thing in Runtime (WOT vocabulary)

    Composition:
    • Aggregations hold Block Instances as children
    • Instance identity uses bml:id (not the type FQN)
    • Nested paths compose with :: (wall::bottom::inner)
    • bml:version pins external references to exact library versions

    Instance serialization (authoring):
    • On Block definitions — &lt;composition&gt; nested inside the aggregation member fills defaults
    • On Block instances — aggregations are filled directly by member name (no composition wrapper);
      property/capability values as attributes or child elements; Aggregation children as nested
      elements (or bml:container for shared directives)
    • readOnly effective members must not be authored on instances → BML_READONLY_MEMBER_WRITE
    • Empty child meaning depends on aggregation type (instance vs TypeReferenceEntity sugar) —
      doc:CompositionModel / doc:SubBlockModel

    Context-dependent interpretation:
    • ValueTypes as type-classes for Property values and Capability input/output
    • Languages as meta-classes for implementation contracts
    • Sub-Blocks in the same .bml file as nested classes (derived types, inner scope)
    • Presets as named default bindings on a class

    Relationships: Library contains Blocks; Block has Members, Presets, Compositions; Catalog
    contains top-level composite Blocks.
  </documentation>

  <references>
    <see type="doc:CompositionModel" relation="SeeAlso">
      Aggregation fill — definition composition vs instance children.
    </see>
    <see type="doc:PartialMemberOverrides" relation="SeeAlso">
      readOnly — instance authoring forbidden.
    </see>
    <see type="core:Block" relation="Extends">
      Root type — abstract definition; not used directly as composition instance type.
    </see>
    <see type="def:BlockDefinition" relation="Defines">
      Meta-model for block definition documents.
    </see>
    <see type="doc:DefinitionModel" relation="ReadNext">
      Next reading step — how definitions describe types and support reflection.
    </see>
  </references>

</block>
